<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Tmutarakan</h1><p class="st-meta">988 CE – 1094 CE (approx.) · capital Tmutarakan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A remote outpost of Kievan Rus' on the Taman peninsula, controlling the strait between the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tmutarakan grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> town of Tamatarkha, on the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermonassa">Hermonassa</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a> broke Khazar power in the 960s, the Rus' took control, and around 988 Vladimir I gave the town to his son Mstislav as his own principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasogs">Kasogs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> lived by trade between the steppe, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. Mstislav defeated the Kasog prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rededya">Rededya</a> in single combat in 1022, and in 1024 beat his brother Yaroslav at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-listven">Listven</a>, dividing Rus' along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. The Tmutarakan Stone records Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gleb-sviatoslavich">Gleb</a> measuring the frozen strait in 1068.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tmutarakan served as a refuge for exiled princes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oleg-sviatoslavich">Oleg Sviatoslavich</a>, who left it in 1094 to fight for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>. Cut off by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, it disappears from Rus' history afterwards, probably passing to Byzantine control. Its location was rediscovered in the 18th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-tmutarakan.jpg" alt="Principality of Tmutarakan" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Kmorozov · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermonassa_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>988 CE – 1094 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tmutarakan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mstislav of Tmutarakan, Gleb Sviatoslavich, Oleg Sviatoslavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tmutorakan, Tamatarkha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1041&amp;focus=principality-of-tmutarakan" aria-label="Principality of Tmutarakan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5553 582 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Tmutarakan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5873 838L6131 811M5873 838L5697 681"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6131 811h0M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5873 838h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5873 838h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1041&amp;focus=principality-of-tmutarakan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
